    "created_at": "2021-11-21T10:55:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-11-21T10:55:17Z",
    "body": "> Thank you for the concrete example for where conditionals could come in handy. Balancing between keeping configuration simple and allowing for powerful configuration has been on my mind lately. I think that, instead of extending the Starship DSL, it may be best if we add support for proper scripting for more advanced configuration using logical operators.\r\n> \r\n> If we add if/else, we'll eventually need logical and/or/not operators, then we'll need to add comments to explain complex logic, and so on. We'd be gradually rebuilding a simple language.\r\n> \r\n> I think #2502 is headed in the right direction in this regard. smile Perhaps not having a scripting language to replace `starship.toml`, but per-module opt-in scripting could be the solution.\r\n\r\nI see, I can certainly agree with that approach as long as (at least a subset of) the Lua script blocks can be entered into `starship.toml` for some simple conditions. Having to refer to a separate file for basic conditions might be a little bit bothersome, at least for me personally :)",
